# HTTP response headers, served by Cloudflare Pages.
#
# This file is the reason the frontend left GitHub Pages. Pages could serve
# files and nothing else — it has no mechanism for setting a response header at
# all — so the Content-Security-Policy had to live in a <meta> tag, and
# `frame-ancestors` and `Strict-Transport-Security` are both ignored there by
# specification. For a tool whose entire pitch is that the server never sees
# your plaintext, "we cannot send security headers" was a real gap rather than
# a nitpick. Cloudflare Pages reads this file and sends them.
#
# The <meta> CSP in index.html and app.html is deliberately KEPT. Two policies
# are intersected, not overridden, so the header cannot loosen the meta tag —
# and the meta tag is what protects the pages when they are served by something
# that is not Cloudflare: `npm run serve`, tools/csp-check.mjs, the Tauri
# webview, and anyone self-hosting the static site behind their own proxy.
#
# !! The policy below must stay character-identical to the meta tag apart from
# `frame-ancestors`. They intersect, so a directive that is stricter in only one
# of them produces a policy that no single file in the repo describes, and the
# violation shows up in a browser rather than in a check. tools/site-check.mjs
# asserts the two agree. !!

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; img-src 'self' data: blob:; connect-src 'self' https://realtimeclipboard.fastapicloud.dev wss://realtimeclipboard.fastapicloud.dev http://127.0.0.1:8000 ws://127.0.0.1:8000; require-trusted-types-for 'script'; trusted-types realtimeclipboard
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=(), interest-cohort=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin

# The service worker is the update mechanism for everything else, so it is the
# one file that must never be served from a cache. tools/build.mjs stamps
# VERSION into it per deploy specifically so that a byte-different sw.js reaches
# the browser and triggers the update; a cached sw.js turns that whole design
# into a no-op and pins a returning visitor to a stale shell indefinitely.
#
# Note this is the BROWSER cache. Cloudflare Pages purges its own edge cache on
# every deploy, so the edge is not the risk here — the risk is the 24 hours a
# browser is allowed to reuse a worker script without asking.
/sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# Read at runtime by src/ui/whatsNew.js, which compares it against the version
# the user last saw. A stale copy means the release notes for the build they are
# currently running do not exist yet as far as the app is concerned.
/changelog.json
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/CHANGELOG.md
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# The HTML names the bundle paths, so a stale document points at assets from the
# previous deploy. Revalidation is cheap — these are a few KB and almost always
# answer 304.
/*.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
/manifest.webmanifest
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
